1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,920
In 1928, Alexander Fleming noticed a mould growing

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:05,920
on one of his culture dishes.

3
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:08,800
Around it, the bacteria had died, leaving a

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:10,160
clear ring.

5
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:12,480
That mould led to penicillin.

6
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:14,960
But penicillin does nothing for the flu, for

7
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:18,080
tinea, for malaria or for a tapeworm.

8
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:20,720
So what makes a disease infectious, and why

9
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,880
does the kind of pathogen decide how we

10
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:23,760
can fight it?

11
00:00:24,967 --> 00:00:28,086
An infectious disease is caused by a pathogen,

12
00:00:28,167 --> 00:00:30,886
an organism or agent that lives in or

13
00:00:31,127 --> 00:00:34,726
on another organism, its host, and harms it.

14
00:00:35,047 --> 00:00:37,366
Because the pathogen can pass from one host

15
00:00:37,407 --> 00:00:39,686
to the next, the disease can spread.

16
00:00:40,407 --> 00:00:44,526
Tuberculosis is infectious: a bacterium causes it, and

17
00:00:44,567 --> 00:00:46,086
it passes between people.

18
00:00:46,727 --> 00:00:48,646
Type 2 diabetes isn't.

19
00:00:49,047 --> 00:00:51,606
It comes from genes and lifestyle, so you

20
00:00:51,687 --> 00:00:52,486
can't catch it.

21
00:00:52,967 --> 00:00:55,286
And most microbes aren't pathogens.

22
00:00:55,687 --> 00:00:58,886
Your gut carries trillions of bacteria, and most

23
00:00:58,967 --> 00:01:00,886
do no harm, or even help.

24
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:05,220
Biologists sort pathogens with a few questions.

25
00:01:05,460 --> 00:01:07,540
First, is it made of cells?

26
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:11,780
Bacteria, fungi and protozoa are single cells or

27
00:01:11,940 --> 00:01:14,660
fine threads of cells, each too small to

28
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:17,540
see on its own, so they're microorganisms.

29
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:20,660
Worms and ticks are animals made of many

30
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:23,700
cells, usually big enough to see, so they're

31
00:01:23,940 --> 00:01:24,900
macroorganisms.

32
00:01:25,540 --> 00:01:29,140
Viruses, viroids and prions aren't cells at all.

33
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:32,820
They're non-cellular, and they can only multiply inside

34
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:33,620
a host.

35
00:01:34,834 --> 00:01:38,913
Inside the microorganisms, ask a second question: does

36
00:01:38,994 --> 00:01:40,713
the cell have a nucleus?

37
00:01:40,994 --> 00:01:42,753
A bacterium doesn't.

38
00:01:42,834 --> 00:01:45,553
It's a prokaryote, with its DNA lying in

39
00:01:45,634 --> 00:01:47,153
the cytoplasm.

40
00:01:47,234 --> 00:01:50,153
The tuberculosis bacterium has a wall containing a

41
00:01:50,194 --> 00:01:53,633
material called peptidoglycan, and is a rod a

42
00:01:53,714 --> 00:01:56,073
few thousandths of a millimetre long.

43
00:01:56,514 --> 00:01:59,393
Fungi and protozoa do have a nucleus, like

44
00:01:59,474 --> 00:02:00,833
our own cells.

45
00:02:00,874 --> 00:02:03,873
Fungi grow as threads or yeast cells, with

46
00:02:03,954 --> 00:02:07,633
walls containing chitin, and spread by spores.

47
00:02:07,674 --> 00:02:10,273
Tinea, a skin infection, is caused by a

48
00:02:10,354 --> 00:02:11,193
fungus.

49
00:02:11,554 --> 00:02:14,273
Protozoa are single cells with no wall.

50
00:02:14,674 --> 00:02:18,753
Plasmodium, the protozoan that causes malaria, lives inside

51
00:02:18,834 --> 00:02:19,873
red blood cells.

52
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:23,940
Macroorganisms are parasitic animals.

53
00:02:24,220 --> 00:02:27,900
Helminths are parasitic worms, and many live inside

54
00:02:27,940 --> 00:02:28,780
the host.

55
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:31,260
The pork tapeworm hooks onto the wall of

56
00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:33,660
the gut and can grow to several metres

57
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:34,420
long.

58
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:37,580
Other parasites live on the outside, like ticks

59
00:02:37,660 --> 00:02:38,300
and lice.

60
00:02:38,940 --> 00:02:43,420
Ecto means outside and endo means inside, so

61
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:48,300
ticks are ectoparasites and tapeworms are endoparasites.

62
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:51,580
Now zoom in to a smaller example.

63
00:02:52,060 --> 00:02:56,220
A virus is genetic material, DNA or RNA,

64
00:02:56,300 --> 00:02:57,820
inside a protein coat.

65
00:02:58,300 --> 00:03:01,340
It has no cytoplasm and no ribosomes, so

66
00:03:01,420 --> 00:03:03,180
it can't make anything itself.

67
00:03:03,740 --> 00:03:05,820
It gets into a host cell and makes

68
00:03:05,900 --> 00:03:08,260
that cell build new viruses.

69
00:03:08,700 --> 00:03:11,500
The virus that causes COVID-19 is about a

70
00:03:11,620 --> 00:03:15,100
ten-thousandth of a millimetre across, so dozens would

71
00:03:15,180 --> 00:03:17,060
fit along one bacterium.

72
00:03:17,420 --> 00:03:21,180
Plants get viruses too, like tobacco mosaic virus,

73
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:23,580
a rod of RNA in a protein coat.

74
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:28,480
Two non-cellular pathogens are even simpler, and easy

75
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:29,200
to mix up.

76
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:32,000
A viroid is just a small circle of

77
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:34,240
RNA, with no protein coat.

78
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:38,080
One makes potato tubers grow long and spindle-shaped.

79
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:41,600
A prion is the opposite: a protein with

80
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:44,080
no DNA or RNA at all.

81
00:03:44,560 --> 00:03:47,040
It's a misfolded copy of a normal protein

82
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:48,960
found especially on nerve cells.

83
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:51,760
When it meets normal copies, it makes them

84
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:54,640
fold the same wrong way, like one bad

85
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:56,080
apple spoiling the rest.

86
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:59,280
Prions cause mad cow disease in cattle.

87
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,960
So a viroid is RNA only, and a

88
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:04,960
prion is protein only.

89
00:04:06,167 --> 00:04:08,646
Plants are hosts too, and most of the

90
00:04:08,727 --> 00:04:10,206
same groups attack them.

91
00:04:10,487 --> 00:04:13,446
A bacterium causes crown gall, a swelling on

92
00:04:13,527 --> 00:04:14,646
stems.

93
00:04:14,687 --> 00:04:17,446
A fungus causes wheat stem rust.

94
00:04:17,607 --> 00:04:21,366
Tiny roundworms called root-knot nematodes swell plant roots

95
00:04:21,487 --> 00:04:22,806
into knots.

96
00:04:22,847 --> 00:04:25,206
And there are two exceptions to remember: the

97
00:04:25,287 --> 00:04:28,806
known viroids infect plants, and the known prions

98
00:04:28,967 --> 00:04:30,246
infect animals.

99
00:04:31,467 --> 00:04:33,866
So why does the group matter so much?

100
00:04:34,427 --> 00:04:36,266
Because a drug has to hit something the

101
00:04:36,347 --> 00:04:38,586
pathogen has and the host doesn't.

102
00:04:39,067 --> 00:04:42,666
Antibiotics like penicillin act on parts only bacteria

103
00:04:42,747 --> 00:04:46,186
have, such as the peptidoglycan wall and ribosomes

104
00:04:46,347 --> 00:04:47,786
shaped differently from ours.

105
00:04:48,267 --> 00:04:51,546
A virus has neither, so antibiotics can't touch

106
00:04:51,627 --> 00:04:53,226
it, which is why they don't treat the

107
00:04:53,307 --> 00:04:53,706
flu.

108
00:04:54,427 --> 00:04:57,386
Fungi and protozoa have a nucleus like ours,

109
00:04:57,467 --> 00:04:59,386
so they need their own drugs, such as

110
00:04:59,467 --> 00:05:02,906
antifungals and antimalarials, aimed at their differences.

111
00:05:03,467 --> 00:05:06,106
And prions, with no genes to target, have

112
00:05:06,267 --> 00:05:07,386
no known cure.

113
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:10,680
Sort any pathogen in order.

114
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:12,480
Is it made of cells?

115
00:05:12,680 --> 00:05:15,160
If so, is it a many-celled animal?

116
00:05:15,640 --> 00:05:17,080
Does it have a nucleus?

117
00:05:17,320 --> 00:05:19,160
And if it isn't a cell, is it

118
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:22,480
RNA only, protein only, or genes in a

119
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:23,240
coat?

120
00:05:23,560 --> 00:05:27,080
Then remember: not a cell means antibiotics are

121
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:28,040
useless.

122
00:05:29,267 --> 00:05:30,706
Two exam questions now.

123
00:05:31,187 --> 00:05:31,666
First.

124
00:05:32,307 --> 00:05:35,506
Three agents were isolated from diseased organisms.

125
00:05:35,987 --> 00:05:38,306
Agent P is a single cell with its

126
00:05:38,387 --> 00:05:41,426
own nucleus and no cell wall, found inside

127
00:05:41,507 --> 00:05:43,106
a bird's red blood cells.

128
00:05:43,747 --> 00:05:46,626
Agent Q is a small circle of RNA

129
00:05:46,787 --> 00:05:50,466
with no protein coat, from deformed potato tubers.

130
00:05:50,867 --> 00:05:53,906
Agent R is a flat, segmented worm two

131
00:05:54,067 --> 00:05:56,306
metres long, from a dog's intestine.

132
00:05:57,027 --> 00:06:00,386
Classify each agent, and justify each answer.

133
00:06:00,707 --> 00:06:01,986
Pause here to answer.

134
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:16,080
P is a protozoan, a microorganism, because it's

135
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:18,720
one cell with a nucleus and no wall.

136
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:22,640
Q is a viroid, which is non-cellular, because

137
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:25,600
it's RNA with no protein coat.

138
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:29,000
R is a helminth, a macroorganism, because it's

139
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:31,440
a many-celled animal, a worm.

140
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:34,400
Calling Q a small virus is the classic

141
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:37,360
mistake: a virus has a protein coat.

142
00:06:38,567 --> 00:06:39,126
Second.

143
00:06:39,607 --> 00:06:43,526
Bovine tuberculosis is caused by a rod-shaped bacterium.

144
00:06:44,007 --> 00:06:47,526
Mad cow disease, or BSE, is caused by

145
00:06:47,567 --> 00:06:48,326
a prion.

146
00:06:48,887 --> 00:06:51,326
Explain why an antibiotic could act on the

147
00:06:51,367 --> 00:06:54,246
pathogen causing one of these diseases, but not

148
00:06:54,327 --> 00:06:54,806
the other.

149
00:06:55,447 --> 00:06:56,806
Pause here to answer.

150
00:07:07,234 --> 00:07:10,593
The antibiotic could act on the tuberculosis bacterium,

151
00:07:10,714 --> 00:07:13,313
because it's a prokaryotic cell with targets that

152
00:07:13,394 --> 00:07:17,073
only bacteria have, such as bacterial ribosomes and

153
00:07:17,154 --> 00:07:19,193
the enzymes that build its wall.

154
00:07:19,554 --> 00:07:22,353
The prion is a misfolded protein, with no

155
00:07:22,434 --> 00:07:25,953
cell, wall or ribosomes, so the antibiotic has

156
00:07:26,114 --> 00:07:27,073
nothing to act on.

157
00:07:27,714 --> 00:07:29,473
Saying the prion is too small for the

158
00:07:29,594 --> 00:07:31,953
drug to reach is the classic mistake.

159
00:07:33,167 --> 00:07:36,126
So when a question names a disease, first

160
00:07:36,207 --> 00:07:39,566
ask what causes it: a pathogen, or something

161
00:07:39,647 --> 00:07:40,046
else.

162
00:07:40,687 --> 00:07:43,326
Then sort the pathogen with the four questions.

163
00:07:43,807 --> 00:07:45,886
Its group tells you what can treat it.

164
00:07:46,527 --> 00:07:49,646
Next, how scientists first proved that a particular

165
00:07:49,727 --> 00:07:52,286
microbe causes a particular disease.

166
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:54,940
Here are the key notes.

167
00:07:55,180 --> 00:07:57,100
Pause the video if you need more time

168
00:07:57,180 --> 00:07:57,980
to copy them.
